Research Paper: Predictive Modeling and Risk Scoring for Bank Customer Churn
Title
Predictive Modeling and Risk Scoring for Bank Customer Churn Using Machine Learning and Explainable AI

Abstract
Customer churn is a major challenge in the banking sector as it directly impacts revenue, customer lifetime value, and long-term profitability. This study develops a predictive churn intelligence system using machine learning techniques to identify customers at risk of leaving the bank. The dataset contains 10,000 customer records with demographic, financial, and behavioral attributes. Multiple machine learning models including Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, and XGBoost were evaluated. XGBoost achieved the best predictive performance and was selected as the final model. Feature engineering and SHAP-based explainability were incorporated to improve model interpretability. The proposed system generates churn probabilities and risk scores that support proactive customer retention strategies.

Keywords: Customer Churn, Banking Analytics, Machine Learning, XGBoost, SHAP, Risk Scoring

1. Introduction
Customer retention is significantly more cost-effective than acquiring new customers. In the banking industry, customer churn can result in revenue loss, reduced cross-selling opportunities, and decreased market competitiveness.

Traditional churn analysis focuses on identifying reasons behind past churn behavior. However, modern banking requires predictive systems capable of identifying high-risk customers before they leave. This project aims to develop a predictive churn intelligence system that provides actionable insights for customer retention.

2. Problem Statement
Banks possess extensive customer-level data but often lack:

Accurate churn prediction systems

Quantitative risk scoring mechanisms

Explainable insights into churn drivers

Consequently, retention campaigns become reactive, broad, and inefficient.

3. Objectives
Primary Objectives
Predict customer churn with high accuracy

Generate customer churn probability scores

Identify major churn drivers

Secondary Objectives
Improve model interpretability

Reduce false churn alerts

Enable scenario-based risk analysis

4. Dataset Description
The dataset contains 10,000 customer records.

Feature	Description
CreditScore	Customer creditworthiness
Geography	France, Germany, Spain
Gender	Male/Female
Age	Customer age
Tenure	Years with bank
Balance	Account balance
NumOfProducts	Number of products
HasCrCard	Credit card ownership
IsActiveMember	Activity status
EstimatedSalary	Estimated salary
Exited	Churn Indicator
Target Variable:

Exited

1 = Churned

0 = Retained

5. Data Preprocessing
The following preprocessing steps were performed:

Data Cleaning
Removed CustomerId

Removed Surname

Removed Year column

Verified missing values

Feature Encoding
One-Hot Encoding for Geography

Binary Encoding for Gender

Feature Engineering
Created additional variables:

BalanceSalaryRatio

ProductDensity

EngagementProduct

AgeTenure

These engineered features enhanced model learning and predictive power.

6. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Churn Distribution
Approximately 20% of customers exited the bank while 80% remained.

Insight
The dataset is moderately imbalanced, requiring stratified train-test splitting.

Geography Analysis
Germany exhibited the highest churn rate among all regions.

Insight
Geographical factors significantly influence customer retention behavior.

Gender Analysis
Female customers displayed a slightly higher churn tendency compared to male customers.

Age Analysis
Older customers were more likely to churn.

Insight
Age emerged as one of the strongest churn predictors.

Correlation Analysis
Correlation heatmaps revealed relationships between:

Age and churn

Activity status and churn

Product ownership and churn

7. Methodology
Train-Test Split
Training Set: 80%

Testing Set: 20%

Stratified Sampling Applied

Models Developed
Logistic Regression
Used as baseline model due to interpretability.

Decision Tree
Provides rule-based predictions.

Random Forest
Reduces overfitting through ensemble learning.

XGBoost
Gradient boosting model selected as the advanced model.

8. Model Evaluation
The following metrics were used:

Accuracy

Precision

Recall

F1 Score

ROC-AUC

Model Comparison
Model	Accuracy
Logistic Regression	(Your Result)
Decision Tree	(Your Result)
Random Forest	(Your Result)
XGBoost	(Your Result)
Best Model
XGBoost achieved the highest overall performance and was selected as the final model.

9. Churn Risk Scoring
The selected XGBoost model generates churn probabilities ranging from 0 to 1.

Risk categories were defined as:

Probability	Risk Level
0 – 0.30	Low Risk
0.30 – 0.60	Medium Risk
0.60 – 1.00	High Risk
This risk segmentation allows targeted retention efforts.

10. Explainability and Feature Importance
SHAP analysis was conducted to explain model predictions.

Major churn drivers identified:

Age

IsActiveMember

Balance

NumOfProducts

Geography

CreditScore

Business Insight
Inactive customers with fewer products and higher balances were more likely to churn.

11. Recommendations
Based on model findings:

Customer Engagement Programs
Increase customer activity through personalized campaigns.

Product Bundling
Encourage customers to use multiple banking products.

Loyalty Rewards
Offer retention incentives for high-risk customers.

Region-Specific Strategies
Implement specialized retention programs in high-churn regions.

Predictive Monitoring
Deploy the churn prediction model for continuous monitoring.

12. Conclusion
This project successfully developed a predictive churn intelligence system using machine learning techniques. XGBoost demonstrated superior performance in churn prediction while SHAP analysis provided transparency and interpretability. The proposed system enables proactive customer retention and data-driven decision-making.

